 #uuid "1e0d1c80-eb5c-5bda-b21b-a06adaec16d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262837 #edb/ref 1074 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩=7$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-3,1,-2⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ -3{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}-2{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1071 "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa3bed00-1af4-5c46-8c12-264209634c51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262846 #edb/ref 1074 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1} & ={t}_{2}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨3,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262857 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262857 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262857 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(-1,1,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5-3t \\\\ y=2+4t \\\\ z=-3+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf1b6add-bb69-5029-a7d5-1bf17a1142b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262857 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-1,1,2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=5-3t \\\\ y=2+4t \\\\ z=-3+t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨5,2,-3⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨-3,4,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ & =⟨-1,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(4,-2,3)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7885abf5-baa7-59dd-be20-bec3451c5be4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262859 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+2t \\\\ y=3-2t \\\\ z=5+t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨2,-2,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302631 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302631 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302631 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1aa5c8f6-bf83-5bd8-9bb3-80811ef65a96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302860 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302860 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302860 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "407d1ed1-6ab5-5fde-b2e9-9ef394359c42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302878 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302878 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302878 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42cce788-2ef2-5bc9-89b5-61bb8342b033" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302924 #edb/ref 1080 "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302924 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302924 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a24ce80-8c12-5476-97ef-85e573270172" #edb/ref 1759218604